-- title:
--   Compiled arithmetic expressions evaluate correctly
-- statement:
--   For every arithmetic expression $e$ over the 64 registers (built from register tops, literals, $+$, negation, selection on sign, multiplication, shifts, floor division, …) that does not mention the eight scratch stacks $56,\dots,63$ (`e.Clean`) and is defined in state $s$ (`e.ok s`), the compiled code `e.code zX` runs from $s$ to the state obtained by pushing the value `e.eval s` on stack $56$, within cost `e.cost s`.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** `IRuns I iprog p s s' c` is the item-language big-step semantics with cost bound $c$; `zX` is the record of scratch stacks used by the expression compiler. The machine layer: `IState HS` is a state with $64$ stacks of items (`Item` = integer or mark), an input position and an output; `RM` programs (straight-line register code `RS`, loops `rep`, conditionals, calls of the multiplication procedure through a `MulSpec` $M$ giving its domain and cost) have a functional semantics `p.run s`, a definedness predicate `p.ok M s` and a cost `p.cost M s`; `Spec M p s s' c` abbreviates `p.ok M s ∧ p.run s = s' ∧ p.cost M s ≤ c`. `ints L` / `gints U` push an integer list / a list of pairs as items (head = top); `zsz z` is the bit length of $|z|$. `MulBnd M m Φ` says the multiplication procedure accepts all pairs of integers of bit length $\le m$ at cost $\le Φ$. Register names (`GU`, `CR`, `KB`, `XN`, …) are fixed stack indices.
-- source:
--   D. Harvey, J. van der Hoeven, Integer multiplication in time O(n log n), Ann. of Math. 193 (2021), 563–617 (Theorem 1.1: multitape Turing machine multiplication in O(n log n)). Lemma `NLogN.Ex.code_runs` of the Lean development NLogN/HExprRun.lean (lines 53–267), written for the prove2.me integer-multiplication campaign.

